  "answer": "To prevent end-to-end outcome-based optimization from hiding internal reasoning and preventing humans from spotting deceptive or unsafe strategies, it has been proposed to incorporate process supervision with step-level human feedback during training (https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/FFz6H35Gy6BArHxkc/task-decomposition-for-scalable-oversight-agisf-distillation). The mechanism involves supervising and rewarding a model's intermediate reasoning steps instead of only its final outcomes, keeping the model transparent and aligned (https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/FFz6H35Gy6BArHxkc/task-decomposition-for-scalable-oversight-agisf-distillation). Specifically, human raters evaluate and mark each intermediate step (such as in a maths scratch-pad) to produce dense, stepwise reward signals, and these labels are then used as rewards or supervised targets to guide the model during fine-tuning (https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/FFz6H35Gy6BArHxkc/task-decomposition-for-scalable-oversight-agisf-distillation)."
